rules_version = '2';

// Deny everything, to everyone, on every path. ADR-006 Paso 5.
//
// This is not a lockdown of a working system — it is the removal of an access path nothing
// legitimate uses any more. Until ADR-006, the backend reached Firestore with the CLIENT SDK,
// so it was subject to these rules and they had to stay open for the server to function at
// all; the price was that the whole database was readable and writable by anyone holding
// firebase-applet-config.json, which is committed to a public repository. That was measured,
// not assumed: an unauthenticated runQuery returned 200 on all nine collections.
//
// The backend now uses the Admin SDK, which authenticates by IAM and does not evaluate these
// rules. No client SDK survives anywhere in the project: `firebase` is out of package.json,
// src/lib/firebase.ts is deleted, no component imports Firestore, and `npm run build` produces
// a bundle with zero occurrences of firestore.googleapis.com. So by the time this rule set is
// deployed, it governs exactly one population — unauthenticated third parties — and denying
// them is functionally a no-op for the application.
//
// A catch-all rather than `if false` per collection. Both close the same door; the difference
// is what happens next. With a catch-all, a new collection is denied by default instead of
// needing its own rule, which makes the tableCloses class of bug structurally impossible: that
// collection was written and listened to for several commits with no `match` block, so every
// write was rejected, the error was swallowed, and the receipts lived only in memory until the
// next restart. Nobody noticed, because the UI reported success.
//
// Two things that keep working, so their failure is not misread as caused by this:
//   - The Firebase console and gcloud. They authorize through IAM, not through rules.
//   - Every read and write the app performs, including the _health heartbeat.
//
// If something DOES break after deploying this, the cause is a Firestore client that was not
// found during the migration, and the fix is to move it to the Admin SDK — not to reopen this
// file. Reverting is one deploy away (`firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` on the previous
// commit) and that is the intended escape hatch, but it reopens the database to the internet,
// so it buys time rather than solving anything.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
